  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\". Reply A is \"```json\\n1.5\\n```\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\". Reply B is \"{\\\"a\\\": 1}\". For Reply A, a code fence holds the float 1.5, so the rule directs the check to the fenced pool; that float is passed over as not scoreable, and the rule does not say to fall back to top-level objects, so Reply A should not return the top-level object {\"a\":1}. For Reply B, there is no fence, so the rule directs the check to the last top-level object, which is {\"a\":1}, and it should return ({\"a\":1}, ()). Thus the rule requires different answers. The check, however, returns ({\"a\":1}, ()) for both, because after the fenced float fails it falls through to the top-level pool. I would be wrong if the rule is read as permitting that fallback, or if the check raises on Reply A instead of returning the top-level object.",
